$ rtok agents sessions --help
What is running in this project: host, provider, model, tokens, start, run time

Usage: rtok agents sessions [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  watch  Redraw the sessions table in place as sessions appear, end or spend
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --all            Also show sessions that have ended
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           JSON instead of the table
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok agents sessions watch --help
Redraw the sessions table in place as sessions appear, end or spend

Usage: rtok agents sessions watch [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --all            Also show sessions that have ended
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok agents usage --help
Tokens and estimated cost per agent and month (or day), from the agents' logs or rtok

Prices come from `[stats.prices]`; a model without one counts in the tokens and is left out of the
cost (`--unpriced` names those).

Usage: rtok agents usage [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)

      --source <SOURCE>
          Data source: `logs` (the agents' own session files, the default), `rtok` (what passed
          through rtok) or `both`

      --host <IDS>
          Only these hosts, comma-separated (`claude,codex`)

      --since <DATE|DUR>
          From a date (`2026-09-01`, whole days in `--tz`) or a duration back from now (`30d`)

      --until <DATE>
          Through this date, inclusive

      --daily
          Bottom table by day

      --monthly
          Bottom table by month (the default)

      --by <AGENT|MODEL>
          Middle table grouping: `agent` (the default) or `model`

      --tz <ZONE>
          IANA time zone for day and month boundaries (default: the system zone)

      --unpriced
          List the models without a price instead of the tables

      --json
          One JSON document

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ rtok agents junk --help
Junk and agent folders: `list` shows rtok's and every installed host's folders with sizes, `clear`
removes rtok's own junk or, filtered, any agent's

Usage: rtok agents junk [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list   Folders, junk kinds and sizes per agent, and the space `agents junk clear` would free
  clear  List what `agents junk clear` would remove; `--yes` applies it
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok agents junk list --help
Folders, junk kinds and sizes per agent, and the space `agents junk clear` would free

Usage: rtok agents junk list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>     User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json              JSON instead of the text
      --bytes             Exact byte counts instead of KB/MB/GB
      --all               Also the hosts that are not installed
      --session-days <N>  Sessions untouched for more than this many days are old, for this run only
      --items <N|all>     Items listed per kind: a count, `all`, or `0` for totals only [default:
                          10]
      --sort <ORDER>      Order of the items in a kind: `size` (largest first), `last-used` (longest
                          unused first) or `path` [default: size] [possible values: size, last-used,
                          path]
      --min-size <SIZE>   Only items of at least this size (`10MB`); on `clear` this also limits
                          what `--yes` removes
  -h, --help              Print help

$ rtok agents junk clear --help
List what `agents junk clear` would remove; `--yes` applies it

Without `--agent`, `--kind`, `--include` or `--older-than` (or with `--agent rtok` alone) only
rtok's own logs and archives: other agents' junk needs a filter.

Usage: rtok agents junk clear [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)

      --yes
          Apply; without it this is a dry run that changes nothing

      --dry-run
          Say the dry run out loud: print what would go and change nothing (the default)

      --stat
          The same preview as the dry run: a removal's line already is its stat

      --json
          JSON instead of the table

      --agent <AGENT>
          Only this agent: `rtok` or a host id (repeatable)

      --kind <KIND>
          Only this junk kind (repeatable)
          
          [possible values: log, archive, cache, temp, logs, build, deps, locks, backups, swap,
          snapshots, index, crash-dumps, sessions, stale-worktrees]

      --include <CLASS>
          Also the review kinds
          
          [possible values: review]

      --older-than <AGE>
          Only items not modified for this long (`7d`, `12h`)

      --session-days <N>
          Sessions untouched for more than this many days are old, for this run only

      --trash
          Move to the OS trash instead of deleting

      --items <N|all>
          Items listed per kind: a count, `all`, or `0` for totals only
          
          [default: 10]

      --sort <ORDER>
          Order of the items in a kind: `size` (largest first), `last-used` (longest unused first)
          or `path`
          
          [default: size]
          [possible values: size, last-used, path]

      --min-size <SIZE>
          Only items of at least this size (`10MB`); on `clear` this also limits what `--yes`
          removes

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

