$ rtok worktree remove --help
Remove your own finished worktree: unlock, `git worktree remove`, delete the branch when merged,
release the claim; refuses dirty or current worktrees, and a foreign lock unless the task is
finished (merged, clean, with commits of its own)

Usage: rtok worktree remove [OPTIONS] <TARGET>

Arguments:
  <TARGET>  The worktree's path, or its task id (the lock's task or the branch `<task>[-<slug>]`)

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>  The rtok agent id (any unique prefix); defaults to `RTOK_AGENT_ID`
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --owner <OWNER>  The owner an old lock names, as `<provider> / <model>`. Defaults to `<host> /
                       <model>` of the agent
      --keep-branch    Remove an unmerged worktree too and keep its branch (a merged branch is kept
                       as well)
      --dry-run        Show the path, branch, size and file count that would go; remove nothing
      --json           JSON instead of the text line
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task --help
This project's tasks: numbered by rtok, stored by the `[tasks]` adapter

Usage: rtok task [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  create    Create a task under the next free id and print the id
  list      The plan: open and in-progress tasks, subtasks under their parent
  show      One task: title, status, parent, subtasks, link and description
  status    Print a task's status, or set it; done and closed move it out of the plan
  next      The first ready task: free or stale, not blocked; a leaf when nothing is blocked
  ready     Tasks that can be claimed, highest priority first
  claim     Claim a task for this agent, or the first ready task when no id is given. On GitHub and
            GitLab the assignee write is last-write-wins, not compare-and-set
  release   Clear the assignee and set the task open. Only the holder, unless --force
  dep       Record that this task waits on another. A cycle is refused and nothing is written
  priority  Set priority from 0 (highest) to 4. 2 is the default and is not stored
  conflict  Record that two tasks touch the same files, so they should not run at once. Symmetric;
            `ready` flags a task whose partner is in progress
  parallel  Mark a task safe to run beside any other task
  sync      Raise the id counters to the adapter's highest ids and report drift; never writes the
            adapter
  export    Mirror the disk tasks one way into an issue tracker; the files stay the source of truth.
            A second run with nothing changed sends no write
  init      Write `[tasks]` into this checkout's `.rtok.toml` and seed the counter from existing
            tasks
  help      Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task create --help
Create a task under the next free id and print the id

Usage: rtok task create [OPTIONS] <TITLE>

Arguments:
  <TITLE>  One-line title

Options:
      --config <PATH>              User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
  -d, --description <DESCRIPTION>  Description: why the task exists and what done means
      --body-file <PATH>           Read the description from this file (`-` for stdin)
      --parent <ID>                Make it a subtask of this task, e.g. `R2` → `R2.1`
      --json                       Print the task as JSON instead of the id
  -h, --help                       Print help

$ rtok task list --help
The plan: open and in-progress tasks, subtasks under their parent

Usage: rtok task list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>    User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --status <STATUS>  Only these statuses (comma-separated: open, in-progress, done, closed)
      --all              Done and closed tasks too
      --parent <ID>      Only the subtasks of this task
      --json             JSON instead of the table
  -h, --help             Print help

$ rtok task show --help
One task: title, status, parent, subtasks, link and description

Usage: rtok task show [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>  Task id, e.g. `R12` or `R2.1`

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           JSON instead of the text
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task status --help
Print a task's status, or set it; done and closed move it out of the plan

Usage: rtok task status [OPTIONS] <ID> [STATUS]

Arguments:
  <ID>      Task id, e.g. `R12` or `R2.1`
  [STATUS]  open, in-progress, done or closed

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --force          Finish a parent even though subtasks are still open
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task next --help
The first ready task: free or stale, not blocked; a leaf when nothing is blocked

Usage: rtok task next [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           JSON instead of the text
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task ready --help
Tasks that can be claimed, highest priority first

Usage: rtok task ready [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           JSON instead of the text
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task claim --help
Claim a task for this agent, or the first ready task when no id is given. On GitHub and GitLab the
assignee write is last-write-wins, not compare-and-set

Usage: rtok task claim [OPTIONS] [ID]

Arguments:
  [ID]  Task id; omit to take the first ready task

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>  The rtok agent id; defaults to `RTOK_AGENT_ID`
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           Print the task as JSON, with `changed`
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task release --help
Clear the assignee and set the task open. Only the holder, unless --force

Usage: rtok task release [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>  Task id, e.g. `R12`

Options:
      --agent <AGENT>  The rtok agent id; defaults to `RTOK_AGENT_ID`
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --force          Release a task held by someone else
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task dep --help
Record that this task waits on another. A cycle is refused and nothing is written

Usage: rtok task dep [OPTIONS] <ID> <BLOCKER>

Arguments:
  <ID>       The task that waits
  <BLOCKER>  The task that must finish first

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task priority --help
Set priority from 0 (highest) to 4. 2 is the default and is not stored

Usage: rtok task priority [OPTIONS] <ID> <LEVEL>

Arguments:
  <ID>     Task id, e.g. `R12`
  <LEVEL>  0 to 4

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task conflict --help
Record that two tasks touch the same files, so they should not run at once. Symmetric; `ready` flags
a task whose partner is in progress

Usage: rtok task conflict [OPTIONS] <ID> <OTHER>

Arguments:
  <ID>     One task
  <OTHER>  The task it conflicts with

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --remove         Take the conflict off instead
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task parallel --help
Mark a task safe to run beside any other task

Usage: rtok task parallel [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>  Task id, e.g. `R12`

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --off            Take the mark off instead
      --json           Print the task as JSON
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task sync --help
Raise the id counters to the adapter's highest ids and report drift; never writes the adapter

Usage: rtok task sync [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --json           JSON instead of the text
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task export --help
Mirror the disk tasks one way into an issue tracker; the files stay the source of truth. A second
run with nothing changed sends no write

Usage: rtok task export [OPTIONS] <BACKEND>

Arguments:
  <BACKEND>  Where to mirror the tasks [possible values: github, linear]

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --dry-run        Print the planned creates, updates and closes and send nothing
      --json           JSON instead of the text
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok task init --help
Write `[tasks]` into this checkout's `.rtok.toml` and seed the counter from existing tasks

Usage: rtok task init [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --adapter <ADAPTER>  disk, github or gitlab (default: disk, or what the file already says)
      --config <PATH>      User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --prefix <PREFIX>    Task id prefix, 1–8 ASCII letters (default: the project name's first
                           letter)
  -h, --help               Print help

$ rtok batch --help
Provider Batch jobs (Anthropic, OpenAI) sent through `rtok proxy`

Usage: rtok batch [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  submit  Create a batch from a JSONL file of provider-shaped requests; prints the batch object
  status  Print the provider's current state of a batch
  fetch   Write the results of a finished batch to a new file
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>  User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
  -h, --help           Print help

$ rtok batch submit --help
Create a batch from a JSONL file of provider-shaped requests; prints the batch object

Usage: rtok batch submit [OPTIONS] <FILE>

Arguments:
  <FILE>  One request per line (Anthropic: `custom_id` + `params`; OpenAI: `custom_id`, `method`,
          `url`, `body`)

Options:
      --config <PATH>        User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --provider <PROVIDER>  Provider whose Batch API to call; default: from the id (`msgbatch_` is
                             Anthropic) [possible values: anthropic, openai]
      --url <URL>            Proxy base URL (default: `[proxy] bind` and `port`)
  -h, --help                 Print help

$ rtok batch status --help
Print the provider's current state of a batch

Usage: rtok batch status [OPTIONS] <ID>

Arguments:
  <ID>  Batch id (`msgbatch_…` is Anthropic, `batch_…` OpenAI)

Options:
      --config <PATH>        User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --provider <PROVIDER>  Provider whose Batch API to call; default: from the id (`msgbatch_` is
                             Anthropic) [possible values: anthropic, openai]
      --url <URL>            Proxy base URL (default: `[proxy] bind` and `port`)
  -h, --help                 Print help

$ rtok batch fetch --help
Write the results of a finished batch to a new file

Usage: rtok batch fetch [OPTIONS] <ID> <OUT>

Arguments:
  <ID>   Batch id (`msgbatch_…` is Anthropic, `batch_…` OpenAI)
  <OUT>  File to create; an existing file is never overwritten

Options:
      --config <PATH>        User config file (else `RTOK_CONFIG` or `<home>/config.toml`)
      --provider <PROVIDER>  Provider whose Batch API to call; default: from the id (`msgbatch_` is
                             Anthropic) [possible values: anthropic, openai]
      --url <URL>            Proxy base URL (default: `[proxy] bind` and `port`)
  -h, --help                 Print help

